#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""apple-plugin-shopping - shopping lists, read from the app that keeps them.

The commands and the JSON say nothing about which app holds the lists. A
BACKEND does: it finds the store, reads it, and knows how to make it
current. There are two, both for Milk for Us (de.danielrinser.milkforus):

  milkforus-server   Pulls from Milk for Us's own sync server, read-only,
                     into a store of this plugin's own. Needs `setup` once.
                     `agent install` pulls every two minutes, and the app
                     never has to run. The default once `setup` has run.
  milkforus          Reads the Core Data store of the iPhone app installed
                     on this Mac. Only as new as the last time the app ran
                     here: `sync` starts it hidden, waits, and stops it.

🛑 THE SERVER IS NOT A PUBLIC API. Milk for Us syncs with Ensembles over
WebDAV at sync.milkforus.com: one baseline file holding every object, and
one JSON file per change set after it. The app gets its WebDAV login from
api.milkforus.com and its HTTP cache keeps that answer, which is where
`setup` reads it. This plugin only lists (PROPFIND) and downloads (GET);
it never uploads, deletes or registers, so it cannot change the lists or
the sync state. Measured 2026-11-02: the baseline plus 102 change sets,
replayed in order, matched the app's own store row for row.

    apple plugins enable shopping
    apple shopping setup          # once: the login, into the Keychain
    apple shopping agent install  # pull every two minutes
    apple shopping items Grocery
    apple shopping history --since 30

`index` is what apple-index calls: one record per list, holding what is on
it and what was checked off.
"""
